  "id": "Compound::DB00967 - Gene::1559",
  "verbalization": "Desloratadine is linked to Cyp2c9 through multiple indirect pathways, all of which involve the histamine H1 receptor antagonists class. The evidence suggests that Desloratadine, as part of this class, binds to Cyp2c9 through a shared mechanism with other histamine antagonists like Azelastine and Diphenhydramine. These compounds, which are included in the histamine H1 receptor antagonists category, are known to interact with Cyp2c9, a cytochrome P450 enzyme involved in drug metabolism. The lowest common ancestor of Desloratadine and its related compounds is the organonitrogen heterocyclic compound, which further supports the notion of a shared biochemical pathway. The presence of multiple independent connections, each through different histamine antagonists, indicates a broader functional relationship between Desloratadine and Cyp2c9, likely involving the metabolic processing of histamine antagonists. This integrated evidence suggests that Desloratadine may influence Cyp2c9 through its interaction with histamine receptor antagonists, potentially affecting drug metabolism.",
